---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import MarkdownToPdf from '@/components/tools/pdf-convert/MarkdownToPdf.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="markdown-to-pdf">
  <MarkdownToPdf client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to convert Markdown to PDF</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Write or paste Markdown in the editor, or drop a <code>.md</code> file to load it. An example document is there to start from.</li>
      <li>Switch to the <strong>Preview</strong> tab to see the exact block structure the PDF will have: headings, lists, quotes, code and tables.</li>
      <li>Pick the page size, typeface, body text size and margin. Each heading level scales from the body size, so one slider controls the whole hierarchy.</li>
      <li>Press <strong>Create PDF</strong> and download.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Supported Markdown</h2>
    <p>
      To convert Markdown to PDF, <code>marked</code> parses the text in
      GitHub-flavoured mode, and the tool typesets the token tree directly. The HTML
      renderer is not used, because the output is a PDF, not a web page.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Syntax</th>
            <th>Rendered as</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code># … ######</code></td><td>Six heading levels, bold, scaled from 1.95× down to 1.0× the body size</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Paragraphs</td><td>Justified to the column with real font metrics</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>**bold**</code>, <code>*italic*</code>, <code>***both***</code></td><td>Bold, italic and bold-italic runs inside a wrapped line</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>`inline code`</code></td><td>Courier</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>- item</code> / <code>1. item</code></td><td>Bullet and numbered lists with hanging indents. Nesting changes the marker</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>- [x] task</code></td><td>Task list with a <code>[x]</code> or <code>[ ]</code> prefix</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>&gt; quote</code></td><td>Indented block with a vertical rule, set in a muted grey</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Fenced and indented code</td><td>Monospaced panel with a hairline border, wrapped and split across pages</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>---</code></td><td>Horizontal rule</td></tr>
          <tr><td>GFM tables</td><td>Proportional columns, bold header, hairline row separators</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>[text](url)</code></td><td>The link text, optionally followed by the target in parentheses</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>~~strikethrough~~</code></td><td>The text, without the strike (standard PDF fonts have no strike glyph)</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>![alt](image.png)</code></td><td>Skipped, and counted so you know</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Raw HTML</td><td>Reduced to its text content</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Why images are not included</h2>
    <p>
      The converter runs in your browser with no network access. A Markdown image
      reference is almost always a URL or a relative path to a file the page cannot
      see, and to fetch it the page would have to send a request. So the tool skips
      images and shows the count before you export.
    </p>

    <h2>Why print Markdown at all?</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>READMEs and changelogs for a release pack, an audit or an appendix.</li>
      <li>Meeting notes and agendas for people who do not want a repository link.</li>
      <li>Technical documentation for a client, as one self-contained file.</li>
      <li>Runbooks and checklists printed for a maintenance window when the network may be down.</li>
      <li>Course material and cheat sheets written in Markdown and shared as PDF.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Code blocks and pagination</h2>
    <p>
      Code is the hardest part of printing Markdown, because it must not reflow and
      must not be cut in half. Here, each block wraps to the column width in Courier
      on a tinted panel. If a block is longer than the space left on the page, it
      splits at a line boundary and the panel continues on the next page, so no line
      is lost. There is no syntax highlighting. Colour in a printed listing usually
      makes it harder to read, and it would need a highlighting engine and a colour
      palette that works in print and on screen.
    </p>

    <h2>Character coverage</h2>
    <p>
      The standard PDF typefaces cover Western European alphabets and common
      punctuation. Text outside that range (Cyrillic, Greek, CJK, emoji) needs an
      embedded font. The converter transliterates what it can and shows the number
      of characters it replaced, so you know if the export is complete. If your
      document has many such characters, print it from a Markdown editor that can
      embed a full Unicode font.
    </p>
    <p>
      Related: <a href="/text-to-pdf">Text to PDF</a> for plain text,
      <a href="/word-to-pdf">Word to PDF</a> for <code>.docx</code>, and
      <a href="/pdf-to-text">PDF to Text</a> for the reverse direction.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
